WEBVTT

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.120
What? Did I wave back to you?

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.620
It wasn't yours, you didn't run over me.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.260
All right, I'll do better the next time.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.220
What's the idea of that book?

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.830
Oh, this little thing I'm picking up.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.340
Oh, Napoleon again, eh?

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.620
You're not drawing paper, that.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.990
Now look here, Donovan, you know what's the problem with you?

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.330
You ain't got no vision.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.660
Now look at me, you know, I ain't always gonna be driving this thing.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.340
You're darn right, you know.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.800
Nothing like to keep your mind on your work on your way, Napoleon.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.840
Reward, Josephine.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:44.560
See you.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.060
Hey, what do you say?

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:46.640
Hop on, huh?

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:47.200
Sure.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:48.980
Come on.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.620
Hey, you got as far as Waterloo yet?

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.140
Oh, now don't tell me. Make me guess.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.160
I wish I had ambitions like you, Looney.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.170
But the best I can hope for is my three squares a day and keep on working.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:14.960
Well, me, I'm different, Fred.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.420
I couldn't keep on going if I didn't think that somewhere something big was waiting for me.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:21.190
See something?

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:21.710
Sure.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.670
In here, I got 348 smackers.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.550
It only took me six years to save it.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.400
Five hundred I'm going to Europe Europe. What are you going to do there? Well, I haven't decided yet. I'll tell you it's going to be something big

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:55.280
Oh, how do you do? Hey, Louie's outside. Where's that letter for?

00:03:58.860 --> 00:03:59.820
Always good

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.320
Who is it?

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:03.130
Syracuse.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.110
Drives a crane out here, but he thinks he's building the canal.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.010
He's got a Napoleon complex.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.970
What can I do for you, Mr. Hawkins?

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.070
I want to meet my new assistant.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:16.380
Bill Henderson of New York, Mr. Littleton Looney of Syracuse.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:17.860
Very glad to know you, Mr. Henderson.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.770
I think you're going to like it around here.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:21.880
There's a letter for you, Looney.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:23.380
Well, who it's from?

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.110
Supposed to open it and find out.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:25.390
Yeah.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:26.210
Good idea.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:28.260
He is registered.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.640
That's bad news.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.260
you know about that you gotta know there's so much money in the whole world somebody left you

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:47.000
money yep 18 000 bucks what it sure surprises me but uncle said he was going to leave all his money

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.930
to the the home for backward children well he kept his work didn't he what are you going to do

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.720
will he go into business business nothing i'm going to europe the land of napoleon europe well

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.070
i'm only going to europe i'm going to germany and isn't he too

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.820
Say, I don't know how we're going to get along with Archie around here.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.980
Yeah, I'm sorry. I feel like a dog leaving before the canal's finished.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.380
Well, I guess we'll just have to struggle along without you.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.410
I don't know if anybody can take your face.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:13.870
Well, I guess you're right.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.990
Well, goodbye, Mr. Henderson. I hope you find everything all right.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:18.790
Yes, very good of you.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:19.430
Goodbye, fellas.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:24.420
Oh, suppose the next time you see me, I'll be wearing one of them monograins in my eye.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:25.510
Bye.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:25.610
All right.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.900
Thank you very much.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.940
A guy like that on one of those big liners?

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:33.810
Hey, fellas, I got a great idea.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:34.910
Now, we've got to go down to New York

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.390
to the engineer's convention anyway.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.440
So, supposedly, leave a day sooner

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.840
and give Looney a big send-off at the boat.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:41.680
Right, right.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:42.560
I'll tell you what.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.560
Send the captain a lot of wires about him, you see,

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.080
and sign big names to him, huh?

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.410
They'll make these stuff out of your port.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.240
And I'll send him a box of cigars from...

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:53.190
from Al Smith.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:53.630
Yeah!

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.320
Say, fellas, he's a nice sort of kid.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:58.960
We don't want to make it too tough for him.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:05:59.800
Too tough for him.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:11.010
Say, before that boat land here, Fred, I'm a hare, sir, I'm going to Europe right away.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:14.820
Gee, that's swell, Looney. I hate to see you go.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.570
Now, what do you say that I'd take you to a trip, huh?

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.760
Not a chance. What did I do with the wife and kids?

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:27.170
Yeah, that's so. Say, do me a favor, will you?

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:27.650
Sure.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.560
Will you go over to get Napoleon off the crane?

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:29.980
Okay.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.830
I'll leave your coat here, Fred. I'll hold it.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:47.270
All right. Here you are, Looney.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:48.170
See, thanks.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.110
Well, so long.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:51.890
So long, Fred.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.470
Always remember that when the skies are darkest,

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.760
the clouds are thickest, beyond the Alps lies Italy.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:18.000
Yes, I'm standing at midnight.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:20.910
Oh, don't worry, Mother.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.390
Of course I'll be all right.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:24.380
Goodbye.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:34.610
Good evening, Miss Ellen.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:36.050
Oh, hello, Mr. Highclose.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.900
I hope it isn't too late to persuade you not to sail tonight.

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:42.870
I'm sorry, but I'm afraid it is.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:48.470
But as I've already pointed out to you, the trip is absolutely unnecessary.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:50.830
Maybe I feel like taking a trip.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.670
But you don't know anything about mine.

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.990
Well, it's never too late to learn.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.070
Who's not inviting toothpaste on the boat?

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:08.020
This attitude of yours leads me to believe that you don't quite trust me.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.240
My father taught me never to quite trust anyone.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.040
Would he have made me your guardian?

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:18.860
Would I have advanced you the money for all the machinery so that the mines might be in operation?

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:22.660
The mines would be in operation if there weren't for some funny business going on that I don't understand.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:26.360
That's just why I'm going to Macedonia, to protect your interest.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.970
I may be young and quite dumb about business, but there's one thing...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:30.860
we've got to get straight.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.010
If the mines aren't in operation by September,

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:34.590
90 days from now, I lose them.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:35.390
That's it?

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:36.230
And you get them.

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:39.510
Well, the syndicate that I represent gets them.

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:41.650
Well, it's just as plain as the nose on anybody's face

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.330
that it's to your interest to have the mines

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:44.890
not in operation before September.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.240
But, my dear child, don't you see that?

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.500
I'm sorry, but that's the way it looks to me.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:53.600
You do misjudge me.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.220
I was so anxious to protect you against, uh,

00:08:59.140 --> 00:08:59.860
absolute loss.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.900
That I persuaded my syndicate to make you an offer.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:05.760
What kind of an offer?

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:12.320
Well, we'll cancel your debt for the machinery and give you $10,000 cash for your interest.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:14.620
$1,000.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.370
And the mines are worth over a million.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.460
I'm sure you won't be overcome if I say no.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:26.980
And it's no use.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:30.940
boy up after my luggage.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:10.750
Well, Looney, you're on the boat at last.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:13.470
Pretty thing, ain't it?

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:14.990
Will you kindly move along, sir?

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:15.290
Yeah.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:16.590
Darson, this is going to be great.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:25.940
Hey, Bill, that guy must be important.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:26.720
Look at that role.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:27.840
Find out who he is.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:28.830
Thank you very much.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:31.640
Good evening.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:32.620
Good evening.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:33.520
Sailing tonight?

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:34.620
I hope so.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:36.460
May I ask your name?

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:37.840
Oh, excuse me, Powell.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:38.820
I'm Senator Powell.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.320
I wonder if you'd mind posing for a picture for us.

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:43.410
Oh, I know, of course.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:47.450
Take off your hat, Senator.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.210
Give us a big smile.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:03.360
What's it got there, body number two?

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:16.840
How about get a guy to take a picture of the moon?

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:18.370
Great, go ahead.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:28.650
Buddy, you don't want to miss this guy.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:30.720
Where is he?

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:33.620
He's one of Syracuse's most prominent men.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:37.130
Right this way, mister.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:37.770
Oh, fellas.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:39.130
That's all right, that's all right.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.370
I believe you always take pictures of people on boats.

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:42.390
Don't act like a hit.

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:44.510
Oh, all right, I'm agreeable.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:45.690
Okay.

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:47.930
Excuse me, gentlemen.

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:49.410
I'm Senator Powell.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:50.700
Glad to know you.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.760
Might I ask who your distinguished-looking young friend is?

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.760
Why, uh, he's one of Syracuse's most famous men.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:58.240
Indeed.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:11:59.970
Uh, might I ask...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.670
Who's anxious to know?

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:06.530
Well, now, I'm a great friend of the captain's, and it might be...

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:07.830
Oh, come on, tell me.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.250
Who is he, eh?

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.020
No, I better not.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.400
Don't tell me that he's Syracuse's most noted son.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.320
Not L.L. Vanderhoff, the famous engineer?

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:23.330
A good guess.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.300
Vanderhoff? Engineer of the new Erie Canal?

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.260
Yeah, but don't tell him I told you.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:29.960
He's probably...

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:36.740
Well, there is genius in shape of that head.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:06.670
Look right here. Look right, Simon.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:11.840
This is a good one.

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:28.660
Put your hat back just a little.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:43.150
Look right here, we didn't get a good one. We want to get a good one.

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.360
Are you ready?

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.890
Well, thank you.

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.360
All right now. Thank you, buddy.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.720
All this to the door.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.480
Now, here we have a few parting words.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:07.280
I'm going to give you this.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:31.360
Thought I had a story.

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:33.730
Maybe the captain would give us some dope on it.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:34.630
Well, there's an idea.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:36.580
Dave, could you tell me?

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:37.640
Excuse me, little lady.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:38.660
Can I be in a service?

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.880
That reporter just said, but I'd like to know if it's true.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:43.080
Well, I'm Senator Powell.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:44.060
I can put your wife.

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.680
Mr. Van Der Hoppe is aboard.

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:46.250
Oh.

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:48.140
He doesn't want to know.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.420
He's traveling under another day.

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:51.010
Oh, please tell me, what is it?

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.520
Now, you'll be discreet.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:53.120
Oh, yeah.

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:54.410
Little, little loony.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:55.500
Little, loony.

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:57.660
I must meet him.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:14:58.780
Thanks.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.100
Yes, sir, boys, I'll never forget Syracuse.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:05.880
She's been like a mother to me.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:07.140
All right, gentlemen, you want me to get a show?

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:07.860
Well, goodbye.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:08.460
Well, goodbye.

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:09.820
Oh, say, I'm a confeder.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:10.680
Oh, yes.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.920
Well, keep your chin up.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:15.640
Oh, I will.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.740
Don't forget old Syracuse.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:18.500
Oh, no, I will.

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:18.680
I will, you.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:19.200
Yeah.

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:36.340
Take a picture of me with a present, sir, will ya?

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:57.520
3D, Senator.

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:31.610
Right kind of friends, eh?

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:33.340
Everyone's been heard from.

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:34.550
But Hoover...

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:35.710
I wonder who he is.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.040
Uh, why, uh, why...

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:41.280
He's, uh, he's really Vanderhoff, the engineer.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:42.840
Is that so?

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:43.300
Mm-hmm.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:45.140
I must have him at my table.

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:48.040
Notify him.

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:56.030
Mr. Highcroft.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.610
And I don't want to see any signs of you falling from his, uh...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.360
But you should have warned me that she was pretty.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.700
Then maybe I would have come in on her side.

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:07.150
Pretty and an heiress to a mind.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:08.780
What a combination.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.560
You've already accepted money from me, so watch your step.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:13.300
Mr. Littleton Looney.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:16.120
Mr. Littleton Looney.

00:17:16.340 --> 00:17:17.440
Littleton Looney.

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:18.890
What a name.

00:17:19.090 --> 00:17:20.870
Mr. Littleton Looney.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:23.450
Well, I suppose we'll have to listen to that for the rest of the trip.

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:24.960
Mr. Highcross?

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:27.170
The Sidney Highcross?

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:29.730
I'm Senator Powell. May I shake it by the hand?

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.980
And your interesting friend, Mr. Pangolus.

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:35.260
Mr. Pangolus, I'm delighted.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:38.080
Well, I feel this is going to be a very pleasant boy.

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:38.660
Yes.

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:42.070
Mr. Littleton Looney, Mr. Littleton Looney.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:43.490
Where is that guy, anyway?

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:45.430
Oh, staying in his cabin, I suppose.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:47.970
Like all geniuses, you know, he's shy.

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:50.600
Genius, not with a name like that.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:51.960
Oh, but that's an assumed name.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:56.590
If you knew who he really is, you'd be impressed, would we?

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:59.970
Yes, I have reason to know the captives would be wise and give me one.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.170
Roundsmith, Otto Kahn, Charlie Swamp, oh they're just like that. Yes? Yes, all asking the captains look after Mr. Looney.

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:15.820
He's a very famous engineer. Oh, excuse me. I am Senator Powell, famous engineer on board.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:22.230
Don't like that so well. Not so hot. If Miss Saunders should get the idea to consult him about her mind.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:29.150
She may not realize who he is. That back-slapping Senator, he is sure to blab it over the whole boat. Nick.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:33.600
I want you to find Miss Saunders and stick closer to her than a twin.

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:34.830
But why?

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:37.430
To keep her from getting friendly with that engineer.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:39.020
Marvelous.

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:40.970
I fly to obey.

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:44.710
Haven't we met somewhere before?

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:46.880
Well, I've no doubt.

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:48.500
I'm Senator Powell.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:49.740
Glad to know you, Senator.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.700
The name's Goodrich, in case you've forgotten.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:52.660
Flo Goodrich.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:54.320
This is my friend, Dolly Clark.

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:55.550
Miss Clark.

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:56.590
Delighted.

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:59.880
Are you two little ladies traveling for your health?

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.920
Oh, we're just two little girls traveling for our wealth.

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:05.520
Frank little girl.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:09.580
Say, who's the guy sitting over there who looks as if he found a spider in his glass?

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:14.680
Sidney Highcross, the banker.

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:16.400
Maybe he needs a cashier.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:17.420
What's his weakness?

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:18.940
He's interested in mines.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:20.950
You better mark him for your play, darling.

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:22.310
You've been digging for years.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.080
No, if you say such awful things,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.840
you might give the sentence the wrong impression of a girl.

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:29.890
I'm not innocent of a man.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.300
what he has but what he is that's the right sentiment little girl don't waste that line on

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:46.720
the senator dolly he's got nothing but a vote any uh real heavy sugar on board senator well

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:53.370
there's a very important person on board traveling in cogito in cogito that means with a beard

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.040
but do go on, Senator.

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:57.260
His name is Littleton Looney.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.860
He's a very famous engineer.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.260
Say, maybe he'd be interested in my bridge work.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:13.880
You two ladies will excuse me.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:15.440
I'll see you later.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:16.400
Oh, I hope so.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:18.090
You certainly will.

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:20.600
Oh, I'm Senator.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.770
A man likes to think a girl has ideals.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:28.450
Oh, can that stuff, Dolly.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:29.950
You can take your hair down when we...

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:32.280
Well, I'm looking for a lifeboat.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.100
That line of yours might sink it.

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:36.420
Don't worry. I'm looking for a guy with enough money

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.440
to give a girl that feeling of safety.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.060
Today Mr. Highcross looks pretty good to me.

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:43.700
I wonder if he needs a secretary or something.

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:45.800
Maybe I'll take a chance with the engineer.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:08.470
Come here.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:35.520
Isn't that nice? This is from one of the biggest men in Syracuse.

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:38.500
Really, sir? Yes, sir. Oh, pardon me.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:45.090
There you are. Thank you very much. Oh, and now me, sir.

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:51.250
Well, servicing it on this skiff here.

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:06.710
Oh, that's very nice, sir.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:08.220
Say, keep in touch with me.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:09.140
Very good, sir.

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:00.740
I'm alright.

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:02.930
I'm chasing Mastodonium.

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:03.820
That's your dog?

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:05.640
I think you're sitting on it.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:12.280
I'm just a man.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.080
I think you like geography, too.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:25.710
Oh, do you?

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:29.570
Yes, yes.

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:32.340
Geography is my favorite subject.

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:36.320
That's why I always wanted to travel.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:41.800
Gee, sure glad I'm making this trip.

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:46.750
Would you like to sit down?

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:51.850
That's a business trip to me.

00:23:57.920 --> 00:23:59.080
Are you going to be on the boat, Mom?

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:01.480
Until it lands.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:05.460
Did you hurt yourself?

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:07.420
Oh, no. It'll be all right.

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:13.150
Are you getting off of Europe?

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:15.580
To Paris.

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:20.280
Funny. I'm going to Paris, too.

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:21.580
That's a coincidence.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:24.930
But now, I've got to go to Macedonia.

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:26.760
Is that in Paris?

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:29.860
You have a sense of humor.

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:32.650
No, I've got some mines there.

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:34.580
Mines? What kind of mines?

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:35.320
Nickel.

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:37.910
Yeah, I thought nickels came from the mist.

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:38.830
And now Mita.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:43.460
Tell me, are you found alone?

00:24:44.260 --> 00:24:45.080
Not exactly.

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:47.000
I'm with Mr. Highcross.

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:49.670
He's a sort of guardian of mine.

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:52.660
I'm really worried.

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:53.400
Yeah?

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:58.630
I have no right to bore a stranger with my troubles.

00:24:59.360 --> 00:24:59.980
Oh, no, that's all right.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.120
All right, I'd like to hear your trouble.

00:25:03.400 --> 00:25:05.610
In fact, if you had a toothache,

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.920
well, I'd be interested in it if it was your toothache.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:12.770
Well, I'll let you know if I feel one coming on.

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:13.670
Is that a promise?

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:15.740
Absolutely. See you in church.

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:31.970
Oh, uh, Mr. Little Looney?

00:25:32.130 --> 00:25:32.290
Yeah?

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:35.010
This package I got for you last night, just before we sailed.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:36.100
Package?

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:37.910
My name's Sight, I'm a purser.

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:40.000
Well, I'm very glad to know you, Mr. Purser.

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:41.540
I have a message for you, Mr. Looney.

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:42.780
The captain's compliments.

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:44.200
Will you dine at his table tonight?

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:44.760
Tonight?

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:46.520
Well, uh, every night, of course.

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:48.940
Why, sure, you tell the captain I'm agreeable.

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:50.210
Ha, ha, ha, ha.

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:51.700
Uh, let's see.

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:56.800
The cigar's from Al Smith.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:25:59.980
Well, smoke on the...

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:00.200
Thanks, Governor.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:01.740
Thanks very much, I will.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:03.480
Allow me.

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:03.990
Yeah.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:34.460
First call for dinner! First call for dinner!

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:14.720
Captain's consummate, sir, and dinner is at 7 o'clock.

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:16.630
Old boy, and ain't I hungry.

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:18.070
Well, let's keep going, sons.

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:19.350
You mean you're ready now, sir?

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:20.850
I'm always ready to eat.

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:22.940
But this is the second night out, sir.

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:24.620
One, two.

00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:25.850
Yeah, that's right.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:27.480
Well, aren't you going to dress, sir?

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:28.880
Well, what's the matter, Mike?

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:30.860
Something missing here?

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:32.860
Oh, no, sir. Everything's all right.

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:35.300
Only people generally wear dinner clothes at dinner.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:36.460
No, no, no?

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:37.360
Surely, sir.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:41.640
Say, son, you better stick with me.

00:27:41.740 --> 00:27:43.940
You know, I need you in my life.

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:52.270
Here, Bill. I hope you got the right thing, sir,

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:54.290
because, you know, I ain't used to this dress and stuff.

00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:56.440
You're always kidding, aren't you, Mr. Looney?

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:57.660
No, no fooling.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.930
You know, one time I bought a two-pants suit.

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:02.860
And I couldn't figure out which pair of pants to wear first.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:06.920
Yes, sir. Do you want to wear your shoes or your trousers first?

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:09.670
Well, whichever is customary to see.

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:17.710
Wouldn't be better. Read that.

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:34.930
Fine. Only I hope he did not tell us it was our instruction that bogged the machinery.

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:37.070
Not Martinez. He's a pretty slick guy.

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:39.050
Mr. Harkless enjoying the old journey.

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:40.350
I was.

00:28:40.370 --> 00:28:44.040
Well, well, Mr. Looney, I believe. Mr. Littleton Looney.

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:45.800
I'm afraid you got me right, brother.

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:46.960
I'm Senator Powell.

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:48.360
Well, I'm very glad to know you.

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:50.060
Where have you been keeping yourself?

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:51.580
Oh, I've been on the upper floor.

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:53.820
You know, I have a feeling that we've met before.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:56.560
Oh, yeah, I remember.

00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:59.100
Did you play Clara Bow's father in that it picture?

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:03.140
Shall we take a little scroll around the deck?

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:04.800
Why, sure it's all right with me.

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.970
Say, then tell me you can buy some swell poster cards over there.

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:13.130
Hmm, so that's Littleton Looney.

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:16.250
Doesn't look much like a mastermind to me.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:19.940
Mr. Highcross, read that.

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:22.270
No bad news, I hope, Miss Saunders.

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:23.560
Not very cheerful.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:36.300
I should say not. I'm afraid this means you'll lose your minds, my dear.

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:41.340
Well, the soldier, as an engineer, I assure you that it would be impossible

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:44.540
to get the mines in operation before the date of foreclosure.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:48.880
Oh, will it? Well, I'll get my own engineer.

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:52.300
There's a very famous one on this boat.

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:54.090
Who, may I ask?

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:56.330
Mr. Littleton Luna.

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:08.520
My dear, I think if you could turn it on, it would be impossible to remove the machinery

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:12.520
before they fall in, because the river is so low at this season.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.990
Very nice girl on board here, and I think that's her back.

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:22.370
Well, listen, if we go around the other way, we'll meet her face to face.

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:24.400
I'm agreeable.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.140
The machinery isn't in operation but in 90 days.

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:33.580
It means foreclosure.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:36.240
You'll get an extension of time.

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:37.820
Well, it isn't up to me.

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:39.600
You see, I'm only one of the syndicates

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:40.440
that advance the money.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:43.090
But, naturally, as your guardian,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.370
I'm anxious to protect you all I can.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:54.300
That's mad from my hand.

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:57.060
Oh, there she is now.

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:57.440
Come on.

00:30:57.990 --> 00:30:59.880
And you need it, man.

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:02.550
I think I have a solution.

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:06.750
It will only take a moment for me to draw up a paper for you to sign.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.690
Just turning your rentals over to me.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:10.500
But I don't see any sense to them.

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:13.250
I'd only lose my minds 90 days before I have to.

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:14.790
I can explain that.

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:16.130
Oh, I've heard quite enough.

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:19.640
And if I lose my minds, I'll lose them in Macedonia

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:23.850
when my 90 days are up for the buggy ride.

00:31:26.610 --> 00:31:27.930
Thanks for the buggy ride.

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:29.960
Beautiful, but not...

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:50.780
Miss Bessie Pritchard, Mr. Littleton Mooney, Miss Bessie Pritchard, Mr. Littleton Mooney.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:51.780
Here you are, a boy.

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:53.060
You're Mr. Littleton Mooney.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:54.700
Do you want to make Bessie Pritchard?

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:58.100
Miss Bessie Pritchard, Miss Bessie Pritchard.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:31:59.640
Oh, you'll make Littleton Mooney.

00:32:04.120 --> 00:32:05.300
Gee, that's a pretty dress you got on.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:07.540
I must speak with you a minute. Won't you sit down?

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:08.470
Oh, thank you.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:13.860
Captain Newt, I started to tell you something about my mind.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:14.320
Yeah?

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:17.000
Well, I'm going to need some expert advice.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:18.700
It's an engineering problem.

00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:19.540
Engineering?

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:21.900
I'm afraid I can't be much for help with that.

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:23.960
Oh, I know you're traveling in Cognito.

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:25.200
Oh, no, first class.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:26.920
Cabin 120, the top floor.

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:28.370
How'd you think?

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.580
It's a different sort of a problem altogether.

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:38.950
I didn't want them to catch you.

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:39.370
Who?

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:42.100
Mr. Highcross and Nick Pangliss, he's an engineer, too.

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:43.330
I know, but listen, I...

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:44.850
You're the only person on the boat who can help me.

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:46.640
Do you want me to help you?

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:49.090
Gee, I'll do anything in the world for you, lady.

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:50.390
Anything in the world.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:52.320
If they should start to pump you,

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:53.940
don't give them any engineering information.

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:55.740
That won't be hard to do.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:58.000
Mr. Highcross is trying to get me to sign a paper.

00:32:58.300 --> 00:32:58.460
What?

00:32:59.220 --> 00:32:59.980
He's trying to get me to...

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:08.900
sign away my mind no you go ahead I feel rather tired

00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:09.630
You really don't know?

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:13.210
Anglis and I are sitting over there having a quiet little chat.

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:14.190
Uncairable.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:17.300
Hi, my partner.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:21.930
What a great idea.

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:35.940
Well, Senator, all ready for the feed bag, eh?

00:35:36.810 --> 00:35:39.490
I, uh, must take my place at the captain's table.

00:35:39.570 --> 00:35:42.150
You can't take us, big boy. We're sitting there, too.

00:35:42.490 --> 00:35:43.130
Oh, indeed?

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:45.660
Yes, a very interesting way to manage New York.

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:49.440
You'd be like a father to me off the castle and look out, the wee girl.

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:51.410
Senator, do me a favor, will you?

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:54.080
Introduce us to the, uh, street party.

00:35:54.180 --> 00:35:56.450
Oh, of course. Oh, Mr. Highcloth.

00:35:57.540 --> 00:35:59.800
Here are two charming little ladies, eh?

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:02.320
How do you do? How do you do? How do you do?

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:05.890
This is really the first time the senator has given me a break.

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:07.130
I hope that you'll enjoy this.

00:36:08.470 --> 00:36:13.460
Pardon me, Mr. Looney. Yes, sir?

00:36:13.460 --> 00:36:16.140
I'd like to shake the hands of the man who built the New Erie Canal.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:17.300
So would I.

00:36:23.790 --> 00:36:26.570
Pardon me, Mr. Looney, but there's a technical question that's been bothering me.

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:29.550
Now, in constructing your canal, did you use dykes?

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:33.020
Oh, no. We used a gang of Italians.

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:34.700
I'm afraid I don't understand.

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:36.840
But what have Italians got to do with dikes?

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:38.940
Well, there's Italian dikes, ain't there?

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:41.010
Well, Dutch dikes.

00:36:41.510 --> 00:36:43.170
Well, everybody has an old taste.

00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:48.220
Of course, personally, I think one Italian with a shovel is worth, well, at least 10 Dutch dikes every day of the year.

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:50.600
Yeah, well, keep in touch with me.

00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:51.060
Thank you.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:59.860
Oh, Mr. Van Dahl, pardon me, Mr. Looney, will you have a cigar?

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:05.490
They come from the lieutenant governor.

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:08.660
Well, hello.

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:37.230
Your table number, sir?

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:39.700
Oh, I'm the captain's table, buddy.

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:43.320
What is your name, sir?

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:46.130
Well, I'm Littleton Looney from Syracuse.

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:48.980
Oh, yes, sir, my certainly the captain's expecting you.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.120
Oh, yes, sir.

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:53.410
Captain Littleton Looney.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:55.520
This is a pleasure, ladies and gentlemen.

00:37:55.870 --> 00:37:57.930
I have the honor to present Mr. Looney of Syracuse.

00:37:58.200 --> 00:37:58.620
Oh, certainly.

00:37:58.940 --> 00:37:59.920
I'm glad to know you all, folks.

00:38:00.860 --> 00:38:04.800
Oh, hello! How you been?

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:10.740
Glad to see you.

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:15.200
Oh, by the way, Mr. Looney, what's gonna happen?

00:38:15.730 --> 00:38:16.150
Thirty-two.

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:18.960
Thirty-two? Whose cabin's that?

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:24.530
Ah, like Bill Rogers, you will have your little guilt, eh?

00:38:24.890 --> 00:38:27.410
Frank and Roosevelt told me that you were a great kid.

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:29.620
Frank Roosevelt?

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:32.460
I could do, too.

00:38:33.430 --> 00:38:35.560
I know all about you.

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:48.260
They're eating over there.

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:57.180
Like snails to me.

00:38:57.860 --> 00:38:58.520
