00:56:39.970 --> 00:56:40.590
Listen, I...

00:56:40.590 --> 00:56:41.270
Miss Ellen, will you listen to me?

00:56:41.270 --> 00:56:44.730
An answer to your inquiries regarding Little Tallulni would say he...

00:56:44.730 --> 00:56:46.310
Miss Ellen, will you let me...

00:56:46.310 --> 00:56:51.260
Would say he occupies a unique position in the engineering world.

00:56:51.690 --> 00:56:54.050
By all means, send him to Macedonia.

00:56:54.490 --> 00:56:58.860
Signed Hopkins, Secretary Syracuse Chamber of Commerce.

00:56:59.500 --> 00:56:59.980
Instruction...

00:57:01.780 --> 00:57:05.230
Well, Mr. Highcross, what do you think of that?

00:57:06.680 --> 00:57:09.880
Wait until we get to Macedonia. There's a real engineering problem.

00:57:10.480 --> 00:57:12.770
Oh, Vanderhoff will take care of that.

00:57:13.110 --> 00:57:15.740
Oh, yes, Vanderhoff will take care of that.

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:34.440
This certainly was a swell idea of yours coming to Macedonia.

00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:37.340
Now, Flo, how did I know we were going to be stuck here like this?

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:39.400
It's not my idea of a lively don't.

00:57:39.520 --> 00:57:43.060
Well, how was I to know that Mr. Looney would keep stalling off the conference of engineers all week?

00:57:43.320 --> 00:57:44.660
Something should have told you.

00:57:44.780 --> 00:57:45.280
Oh, don't be...

00:57:45.560 --> 00:57:48.600
And something should have told you, your friend Highcross is a washout.

00:57:49.130 --> 00:57:51.390
Well, can I help if he keeps worrying about those silly minds?

00:57:51.530 --> 00:57:54.630
Oh, I did my shirt to be on the train for home in the open spaces.

00:57:56.160 --> 00:57:58.010
Oh, see the pretty Rolls Royce.

00:57:58.780 --> 00:57:59.800
Quite a snappy layout.

00:58:01.720 --> 00:58:07.470
Waiter. Waiter, who are the two rather distinguished-looking gentlemen?

00:58:08.080 --> 00:58:13.950
Oh, that is Heffitt, Minelescu, and his partner, Chakinga, Bruce.

00:58:14.240 --> 00:58:15.740
Personally, I'd rather have a baked apple.

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:18.500
They are the two richest men in Macedonia.

00:58:18.580 --> 00:58:19.740
They look very promising.

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:27.290
I think we're being invited to go places.

00:58:27.570 --> 00:58:29.050
I think we're going to accept.

00:58:30.060 --> 00:58:31.100
What do you mean I've done about life?

00:58:31.360 --> 00:58:34.420
People that ride in Rome always need sempre tarot.

00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:40.040
Thank you girl.

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:56.830
You took the words right out of my mouth.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:01.180
Look, it is all set.

00:59:01.440 --> 00:59:03.820
The other engineers, they will be here at three.

00:59:04.130 --> 00:59:04.450
Good.

00:59:05.110 --> 00:59:10.300
Then, Mr. Looney's reputation as a great engineer has just 15 minutes longer to live.

00:59:10.500 --> 00:59:11.840
Charming country, Master Donny.

00:59:12.180 --> 00:59:12.820
Charming deal?

00:59:13.340 --> 00:59:13.640
Yes.

00:59:13.920 --> 00:59:16.800
Rather spoiled by the stalled machinery, don't you think?

00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:18.920
Oh, well, that won't be for long.

00:59:19.510 --> 00:59:21.370
Mr. Looney will take care of that.

00:59:21.530 --> 00:59:22.170
Oh, will he?

00:59:22.660 --> 00:59:23.200
Got a match?

00:59:23.580 --> 00:59:23.780
Yeah.

00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:24.410
Really?

00:59:25.050 --> 00:59:29.010
In just 15 minutes, you will have a chance to see your protégé make a fool of himself.

00:59:29.190 --> 00:59:29.970
Oh, not the end.

00:59:30.030 --> 00:59:31.610
The engineer of the New Erie Canal.

00:59:31.950 --> 00:59:34.030
I'll bet my shirt on one thing.

00:59:34.130 --> 00:59:36.930
The engineer of the New Erie Canal wasn't Littleton Looney.

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:39.030
Well, I told you that was an assumed name.

00:59:39.570 --> 00:59:41.460
Hey, then, uh...

00:59:41.520 --> 00:59:42.660
What's his real name?

00:59:42.900 --> 00:59:44.100
Uh, Abelhoff.

00:59:44.180 --> 00:59:47.410
I don't believe there's any such...

01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:17.090
Quarter past three, Miss Saunders. Are we going to wait for the great genius all day?

01:00:17.270 --> 01:00:17.710
He'll be.

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:22.360
But this group of experts, all busy men, are here to report on the situation.

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:25.000
I'll hear the report then while we're waiting for Mr. Looney.

01:00:25.200 --> 01:00:29.910
Miss Saunders, have you any other plans in case Mr. Looney does not show up?

01:00:30.480 --> 01:00:31.400
He'll be here.

01:00:31.720 --> 01:00:35.060
Very well. Mr. Baudet, will you act as spokesman?

01:00:35.290 --> 01:00:35.930
Anyway, Monsieur.

01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:38.400
Mademoiselle, I haven't much to say.

01:00:39.150 --> 01:00:41.330
It is with great regret that I report our fear.

01:00:42.290 --> 01:00:44.490
We have gone through this problem more thoroughly

01:00:44.490 --> 01:00:46.800
and have concluded that there is no way

01:00:46.800 --> 01:00:48.500
of carrying this machinery to the mine

01:00:48.500 --> 01:00:50.300
until after the September rain.

01:00:50.990 --> 01:00:53.170
But if we wait till September, I lose my mind.

01:00:53.440 --> 01:00:54.720
Why must we wait for rain?

01:00:55.500 --> 01:00:58.700
Because the river is so low that not even flat-bottomed boats

01:00:58.950 --> 01:00:59.890
could carry the machinery.

01:01:00.620 --> 01:01:02.520
Well, Mr. Looney will take care of that.

01:01:02.700 --> 01:01:04.640
Ah, but where is Mr. Looney?

01:01:06.090 --> 01:01:09.610
Mr. Beauvais, will you be good enough to go and see if Mr. Looney is back in his room?

01:01:09.830 --> 01:01:12.090
Oh, it's pleasure. I'm most anxious to meet it.

01:01:21.010 --> 01:01:24.290
Have I the honor of dressing Mr. Looney?

01:01:25.570 --> 01:01:26.190
Quite so.

01:01:26.620 --> 01:01:27.540
I am Pierre Beauvais.

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:29.970
It is a great pleasure for me to...

01:01:30.190 --> 01:01:32.390
who know the man who is responsible for the new Erie Canal.

01:01:37.210 --> 01:01:38.350
Is Miss Saunders in there?

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:40.290
Yes. She's waiting for you.

01:01:42.830 --> 01:01:44.350
Listen, will you tell her to come out in just a minute?

01:01:44.590 --> 01:01:45.290
But the conference!

01:01:46.490 --> 01:01:47.850
I know, but I gotta see her first.

01:01:48.530 --> 01:01:49.190
I think pleasure, Monsieur.

01:02:02.270 --> 01:02:03.810
We're all waiting for you inside.

01:02:04.730 --> 01:02:06.130
Well, will you wait here just a minute?

01:02:13.010 --> 01:02:15.220
I want to talk to you.

01:02:15.440 --> 01:02:16.740
Let's go in the conference room.

01:02:16.940 --> 01:02:18.680
I know, but will you sit down just a minute, please?

01:02:25.110 --> 01:02:29.360
I got something to tell you.

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:38.550
Well, if you remember, if you look back, you'll surely remember that I tried to break this to you before.

01:02:39.320 --> 01:02:40.220
Break what to me?

01:02:41.380 --> 01:02:45.520
Oh, well, I meant to tell you ever since this thing started.

01:02:45.680 --> 01:02:48.230
But what happened, it made it harder for me.

01:02:48.840 --> 01:02:50.000
What are you talking about?

01:03:00.250 --> 01:03:01.770
I ain't, and I never was an engineer.

01:03:06.020 --> 01:03:06.340
Why?

01:03:09.470 --> 01:03:11.430
How could you lie to me like that?

01:03:11.610 --> 01:03:12.630
Oh, I meant to tell you.

01:03:12.790 --> 01:03:13.250
Honest, I did.

01:03:13.490 --> 01:03:15.130
Well, you picked a fine time to do it.

01:03:15.950 --> 01:03:20.910
Well, I've been trying to get up enough courage to tell you about it ever since it began.

01:03:21.400 --> 01:03:22.780
Well, you're just a little late.

01:03:31.330 --> 01:03:34.900
And I'm awful sorry you came all this way to make a fool of me.

01:03:35.410 --> 01:03:36.970
Oh, no, I didn't. That's not the reason.

01:03:37.110 --> 01:03:38.490
Oh, I suppose you came for the ride.

01:03:40.040 --> 01:03:45.540
No, I came because, well, because I thought we liked each other.

01:03:48.990 --> 01:03:50.910
Well, you might have saved yourself the trip.

01:04:00.280 --> 01:04:03.930
You can draw off those papers that you wanted me to sign.

01:04:04.580 --> 01:04:07.160
On the terms we originally discussed?

01:04:07.530 --> 01:04:08.950
Well, I don't care what terms.

01:04:34.250 --> 01:04:38.320
Hold it in, you're a bust. Just a sap from Corsica.

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:44.820
I've met my water town.

01:04:47.320 --> 01:04:48.660
What do you want now?

01:04:48.780 --> 01:04:52.000
Even the greatest of us must be subject to these attacks of nerves.

01:04:52.510 --> 01:04:54.270
The brain is a very delicate instrument.

01:04:54.670 --> 01:04:55.810
What are you trying to do, kid me?

01:04:56.680 --> 01:04:59.660
It's impossible that the man who's conceived the new airy canal...

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:04.020
I have nothing to offend this dilemma, my dear confrere.

01:05:04.340 --> 01:05:06.740
I beg you to remember the new Erie Canal.

01:05:07.320 --> 01:05:09.120
Oh, dry up damn the canal.

01:05:16.640 --> 01:05:18.990
Dry up damn the canal.

01:05:22.660 --> 01:05:23.080
Got it?

01:05:26.610 --> 01:05:28.450
Tablet Saunders, sign right here.

01:05:30.520 --> 01:05:30.880
What?

01:05:31.630 --> 01:05:32.390
You listen to me.

01:05:32.510 --> 01:05:33.070
Give that back.

01:05:33.610 --> 01:05:34.990
Somebody said that river's got a slate bottom?

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:35.970
Well, what about it?

01:05:36.010 --> 01:05:36.470
Yes, yes, a slate bottom.

01:05:36.510 --> 01:05:37.690
Too much water on us some of the time?

01:05:37.810 --> 01:05:38.290
Yes, yes.

01:05:38.370 --> 01:05:39.130
And not enough now?

01:05:39.350 --> 01:05:40.110
No, no.

01:05:40.730 --> 01:05:41.550
Dry upper bottom.

01:05:44.520 --> 01:05:46.660
Dry up the river bottom and you've got a slate roadway.

01:05:48.140 --> 01:05:49.620
It's a bullet for New Eureka now.

01:05:49.960 --> 01:05:51.560
Oh, New Eureka now nothing.

01:05:51.660 --> 01:05:53.100
I don't even know what that darn thing is.

01:05:55.400 --> 01:05:58.400
As a brass boy, I think I'll give him a job.

01:05:58.580 --> 01:05:58.820
Yeah?

01:05:59.300 --> 01:05:59.920
Doing what?

01:06:00.570 --> 01:06:03.630
Engineering? Since when is that your identity?

01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:06.120
Oh, about forty-five years.

01:06:07.480 --> 01:06:09.840
Is he in Vanderhoof?

01:06:10.150 --> 01:06:11.830
But you told us noone he was.

01:06:11.950 --> 01:06:16.770
I found it easier to hide my identity by putting it on somebody else.

01:06:16.850 --> 01:06:17.630
What's the idea?

01:06:17.810 --> 01:06:19.730
Well, I've been retained by your sending it.

01:06:20.650 --> 01:06:21.650
We get a line on you.

01:06:23.030 --> 01:06:23.250
Plenty.

01:06:25.360 --> 01:06:27.250
About ten minutes. Clear out.

01:06:30.060 --> 01:06:32.720
Men shouldn't try to take mine, do we, poor girls?

01:06:33.800 --> 01:06:35.120
Go boil your head.

01:06:35.620 --> 01:06:36.360
I'll be nice.

01:06:37.350 --> 01:06:39.050
I'll have to call a Macedonian cop.

01:07:01.970 --> 01:07:04.210
If you go to the channel, you can tell us more.

01:07:33.870 --> 01:07:36.550
And they lived happily ever after, just like me and you, huh?
